      "explanation": "Method on a referenced project's ParsedCommandLine that lazily builds the two direction maps: sourceToProjectReference (each input/source file path -> SourceOutputAndProjectReference{Source,OutputDts,Resolved}) and outputDtsToProjectReference (each produced .d.ts path -> the same record). It iterates getOutputDeclarationAndSourceFileNames, computes outputDts via GetOutputDeclarationFileNameWorker, and stores records under tspath.ToPath keys; guarded by sourceAndOutputMapsOnce. It is invoked per resolved project reference in projectreferenceparser.go:28."
    },
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/compiler/projectreferencedtsfakinghost.go",
      "symbol": "fileExistsIfProjectReferenceDts",
      "line": 209,
      "explanation": "Tristate FS helper. For a path, it looks up outputDtsToProjectReference; if unmapped it returns core.TSUnknown (not a project-reference dts), but if mapped it returns TSTrue/TSFalse according to whether the real corresponding source file (mapping.Source) exists on disk. Thus an expected .d.ts that was never emitted still 'exists' whenever its source does. It is reached from FileExists -> fileOrDirectoryExistsUsingSource (line 158), with TSUnknown triggering symlinked-node_modules fallback rather than a decisive answer."
    },
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/compiler/filesparser.go",
      "symbol": "load",
      "line": 59,
      "explanation": "parseTask.load is the per-file loading routine. Before extension checks, metadata lookup, and parseSourceFile (which calls host.GetSourceFile -> ReadFile + parser.ParseSourceFile), it consults loader.projectReferences.getParseFileRedirect(t). A non-empty redirect causes t.redirect(loader, redirect) to be installed as the sole subtask instead of reading/parsing the original path."
    }
  ],
  "flow": "The mapper's maps are seeded from ParseInputOutputNames: for each referenced configuration it produces source->output and output->source associations. getParseFileRedirect (projectreferencefilemapper.go:47) uses the useSourceOfProjectReference policy to pick direction: when the program can use project-reference sources, an incoming .d.ts path is looked up in outputDtsToProjectReference and redirected to its Source (with a realpathDtsToSource fallback for symlinked node_modules); otherwise a source path is looked up in sourceToProjectReference and redirected to its OutputDts. This policy is computed once by canUseProjectReferenceSource() (program.go:64) and flows into the builder in fileloader.go:341, so it governs the whole loading flow. During load, the redirect check happens first (filesparser.go:73): if a source path is returned, redirect() creates a new parseTask for that path and sets it as the only subtask. Scheduling then happens in filesParser.start (filesparser.go:313-319): whenever a loaded task has a non-nil redirectedParseTask, loadSubTasks is forced true and startedSubTasks marked, so the redirected dependency is always traversed. Resolution also benefits because getRedirectForResolution (projectreferencefilemapper.go:102) returns the redirected ParsedCommandLine so compiler options/module resolution run under the referenced project. Discovering that the missing output 'exists' is delegated to the faking FS: FileExists -> fileOrDirectoryExistsUsingSource -> fileExistsIfProjectReferenceDts returns TSTrue when the output dts is mapped and its source exists, TSFalse when mapped but the source is gone, and TSUnknown when the path is not a project-reference output at all. TSUnknown is the distinguishing signal: fileOrDirectoryExistsUsingSource only treats non-TSUnknown as authoritative and otherwise continues to node_modules/symlink probing, so an unmapped declaration path is never mistaken for a mapped-but-source-missing one."
}

