  "flow": "Pipeline order. The emitter calls estransforms.GetESTransformer (emitter.go:164); its ESNext case returns Chain(newESDecoratorTransformer, newClassFieldsTransformer), and Chain executes components left-to-right (chain.go:20-22,36-38), so the decorator stage (esDecoratorTransformer) runs over the whole source file before the class-fields stage (classFieldsTransformer). For a derived class targeting ES2015 the same pair is reached through NewES2016Transformer -> ... -> esDecoratorAndClassFields because class fields/private elements are always down-leveled.\n\nDecorator stage. createClassInfo (esdecorator.go:483) pre-scans members: each decorated non-static method/accessor seeds one __runInitializers(this, _instanceExtraInitializers) into ci.pendingInstanceInitializers (lines 531-544). visitPropertyDeclaration (1574) then serializes each decorated field: partialTransformClassElement emits __esDecorate and allocates _x_initializers/_x_extraInitializers; the field value is wrapped as __runInitializers(this, _x_initializers, value) (1615); injectPendingInitializers prepends the queue accumulated from *earlier* members (1646); the member's own extra initializers are appended to the queue only *after* that (1648-1651). Hence for two fields a,b the emitted value is __runInitializers(this, _a_extraInitializers) followed by __runInitializers(this, _b_initializers, value_b) inside b's initializer: a's extra initializers run before b's value, never inside/ before a's own value. This is required because extra initializers are post-initialization hooks attached to a member; they must observe that member already initialized by its value initializers, so queuing them after computing the member's value guarantees the decorator contract and correct ordering among members.\n\nFinal-queue handoff. If the last decorated member is a field, later fields consume the queue. If there is no later field (fieldless class, or the decorated member is a method/auto-accessor whose extra initializers were never drained by a field), visitConstructorDeclaration (1184) calls prepareConstructor (1122), which synthesizes one __runInitializers(this, _instanceExtraInitializers) expression statement and clears pendingInstanceInitializers. That statement is then positioned by transformConstructorBodyWorker (1138) around the super-call path. This is the fieldless variant: a decorated instance method alone still registers extra initializers because createClassInfo pushed the initializer and prepareConstructor drains it.\n\nComputed name single evaluation. A side-effecting computed name is expanded into several declarations (backing field, getter, setter in the decorator stage; one assignment per field in the field stage). Rather than re-evaluate, the transform hoists it into a generated name: esdecorator caches via findComputedPropertyNameCacheAssignment or a fresh temp and uses the assignment for the getter name and the bare temp for the setter name (esdecorator.go:1679-1694); classfields replaces the name with NewGeneratedNameForNode(propertyName) (classfields.go:2743) and getPropertyNameExpressionIfNeeded (2876-2908) emits the single assignment into the hoisted variable/lexical environment (AddVariableDeclaration/AddLexicalDeclaration). The printer's node-keyed name cache (namegenerator.go:138-152) returns the same generated identifier for the same node, so every declaration references one evaluation.\n\nField stage. transformConstructorBody (2503) builds the initializer statement list: private-method branding first through addInstanceMethodStatements (2539), then parameter properties, then ordinary fields (2542-2549); if the class used assignment-style fields (useDefineForClassFields:false) transformPropertyWorker emits this.x = value (2811-2815), otherwise Object.defineProperty descriptors (2817-2832). transformConstructorBodyWorker (2569) inserts these relative to the super call. Private-method branding must precede field initializers because a field initializer may call the private method, and after down-leveling the method lives only in a WeakSet; the WeakSet.add brand must have run first or the call's brand check fails. This ordering is asserted by the comment at classfields.go:2538.\n\nTry-block/super placement. Both transformConstructorBodyWorker implementations recurse into a TryStatement that contains super() (esdecorator.go:1148-1169; classfields.go:2441-2464) so injected initializer statements are placed immediately after super() *inside* the try. Moving them outside the try would change failure behavior: any decorator/field initializer that throws would no longer be caught by the surrounding catch/finally, and inserting before super() in a derived class would touch this before it is initialized. The checkout only guarantees this lexical placement/reuse of the try block; it does not add new runtime receiver semantics beyond what the emitted __runInitializers/WeakSet helpers imply.\n\nCounterfactuals. (1) Reversing the transform stages: the class-fields pass requires decorators already elided (transformFieldInitializer asserts !HasDecorators, classfields.go:994) and relies on decorator-injected __runInitializers wrappers on field initializers; running class fields first would move field expressions into the constructor and strip declarations before the decorator pass could read them, losing both value and extra initializer ordering. (2) Dropping original-node associations: parameter-property detection depends on tx.EmitContext().MostOriginal plus IsParameterPropertyDeclaration (classfields.go:2542-2546, 2785-2786, 2484-2492); without the original link the parameter property would be classified as an ordinary field and the constructed-name branch (2790-2806) could not recover the local binding. (3) Overwriting a parameter-property initializer without preserving decorator effects: the parameter-property branch explicitly unwraps the '(__runInitializers(this, _instanceExtraInitializers), void 0)' comma expression and inlines the __runInitializers call ahead of the local name (2790-2806); a naive overwrite with just the parameter name would silently drop the decorators' added initializers."
